      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To elevate the \"sloth\" argument, frame it around changes in labor supply elasticity, asserting that unconditional income directly lowers the opportunity cost of leisure for low-wage workers. If survival is guaranteed, the incentive premium for taking demanding, undesirable jobs vanishes, leading to mass labor withdrawal in essential sectors. This isn't just laziness; it's a predicted, rational market adjustment where essential services face crippling shortages if labor participation plummets.",
      "source": "target",
